Furthermore, solid-phase catalysis showed that \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) can also rapidly hydrolyze DMNP under high-humidity conditions without bulk water or external bases. This work provides a viable solution toward the implementation of MOF materials into protective equipment for practical nerve agent detoxification.
KEYWORDS: nerve agents, metal−organic frameworks, catalysis, pure water, solid phase
Organophosphorus nerve agents, such as Soman, Sarin, and VX, can easily penetrate human mucosa and interfere with the central nervous system via irreversible bonding to acetylcholinesterase (Figure 1a).1,2 As a result, exposure to even small amounts of nerve agents can cause constant muscle contraction, including the muscles needed for breathing, and ultimately lead to death by asphyxiation.2,3 Since chemical warfare agents (CWAs) were first used during World War I, various methods and materials have been
investigated for the adsorption and detoxification of these extremely harmful compounds.4 Catalytic hydrolysis emerged as a feasible and effective approach for the degradation of nerve agents and many efforts have been devoted to the development of highly active and robust catalytic materials. The discovery of such materials not only is essential for neutralizing large stockpiles of existing nerve agents but also highly active materials can be incorporated into personal protective equipment such as face masks and protective suits.
Over the past decade, metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) have been demonstrated as one of the most promising class of materials for the destruction of CWAs and their simulants.5−10 Particularly, owing to their high catalytic activity and exceptional chemical stability, Zr-MOFs composed of a periodic distribution of Lewis-acidic \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters and carboxylate linkers have emerged as top-performing catalysts for the rapid degradation of nerve agents and simulants (Figure 1b).5,11−14 Several Zr-MOFs displayed high activity for the
hydrolysis of different types of nerve agents/simulants with half-lives of only a few minutes or even seconds. Among them, MOF-808 containing 6-connected \(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\) clusters and benzene tricarboxylate linkers achieved instantaneous hydrolysis of nerve agent GD and VX \(\left(t_{1/2}<1\ \mathrm{min}\right)\) and a simulant dimethyl-4-nitrophenyl phosphate (DMNP, \(t_{1/2}<30\ \mathrm{s}\)) in aqueous alkaline solutions. It is worth noting that most studies investigated the MOF-catalyzed nerve agent/ simulant degradation in bulk water buffered by \(N\cdot\) -ethylmorpholine (NEM). The NEM serves as an essential base to regulate the \(\mathrm{pH}\) and regenerate the active \(Z\mathbf{r}\) sites in MOFs during the catalysis. While this approach holds promise for the catalytic detoxification of nerve agent stockpiles, it is not ideal for use in personal protective equipment given the high volatility of NEM and the large volume of water required in these reactions.
To overcome this problem, heterogeneous bases/basic species have been either simply mixed with MOF particles or incorporated onto the metal nodes of MOFs via postsynthetic modifications. For instance, amine-containing modulators and metal alkoxides were previously incorporated onto the \(Z\mathbf{r}\) clusters of several \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs, which catalytically hydrolyzed nerve agents/simulants in pure water without NEM. Nevertheless, this postsynthetic strategy is limited to only certain MOFs with accessible sites and/or large apertures for desired modification, and the catalytic activities of the modified MOFs in pure water are generally lower than that in NEM buffered solutions. Alternatively, a series of polymeric organic amines were mixed with \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs, affording MOF/polymer mixtures that can degrade nerve agents/simulants in unbuffered aqueous solutions. Very recently, these MOF/polymer mixtures were further integrated onto textile fibers for the hydrolytic destruction of nerve agents/simulants under ambient conditions. This approach requires minimal
synthetic efforts and can achieve fast catalysis in water. However, the selection of effective polyamine base (i.e., linear or branched; low or high molecular weight) is largely dependent on the type of \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs, and the subtle relationship between the polymeric amine base and different \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs remains unknown. Therefore, constant efforts are needed to discover efficient MOF-based catalysts toward the hydrolysis of nerve agents/simulants in pure water and under ambient conditions.
In nature, the phosphotriesterase (PTE) enzyme from soil bacteria is highly efficient in catalyzing the hydrolysis of organophosphorus compounds. The active site of PTE contains a hydroxide-bridged binuclear \(Z\mathbf{n}(\mathrm{II})\) center ligated to residues of histidine, aspartate, and lysine (Figure 2a). The binuclear metal centers facilitate the activation of the organophosphate for nucleophilic attack; the ligated aspartate carboxylate and histidine imidazole are also crucial in the enzymatic hydrolysis mechanism. Specifically, these ligated groups serve as proximate bases in the catalytic cycle, promoting proton transfer and the regeneration of the active binuclear \(Z\mathbf{n}\) centers of PTE. In essence, the binuclear \(Z\mathbf{n}\) center and the surrounding ligands work collaboratively, enabling the extraordinary catalytic activities of PTE. The high cost and poor stability of PTE, however, has limited its practical application in nerve agent detoxification. On the other hand, \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs are very robust solid-state materials that retain their structures and activities under various conditions (e.g., different \(\mathrm{pH}\), temperature, and humidity). In \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs, the \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) cluster resembles the structure of the \(Z\mathbf{n}_{2}\) center in PTE with a \(\mathrm{Zn^{II}{-}O H{-}Z n^{I I}}\) active site and is considered responsible for the MOF's catalytic activity in the hydrolysis of phosphoester bonds. By incorporating imidazole derivatives (which mimic the histidine residue in PTE) into \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs, we can create MOF composites that are structurally reminiscent of the PTE
active sites as well as their surrounding ligands. This bioinspired design will likely lead to new catalysts with extraordinary activities for nerve agent detoxifications.
To test our hypothesis, imidazole \(\left(\mathbf{Im}\right)\) and 2-methylimidazole \(\left(\mathbf{MeIm}\right)\) were encapsulated into the pores of MOF-808, affording two MOF composites \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) and \(\mathbf{MeIm}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\), respectively. These MOF composite catalysts displayed high catalytic activity toward the hydrolysis of a nerve agent simulant, DMNP, in unbuffered aqueous solution. To the best of our knowledge, \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) exhibited the highest catalytic activity among all MOF-based materials toward DMNP hydrolysis in pure water (unbuffered) and displayed a half-life of less than \(30~\mathrm{s}\). Notably, \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) can also rapidly hydrolyze DMNP in solid-phase under high humidity conditions (without bulk water or external bases), and this advancement serves as a key step toward the practical implementation of MOF-based CWA detoxification catalysts.
MOF-808 was synthesized according to the reported procedure,30 and the high phase purity was confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns as shown in Figure 3a. The Im or MeIm molecules were vaporized into the pores of activated MOF-808 to yield \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) and \(\mathbf{MeIm}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\), respectively.31 As shown in Figure 3a, the PXRD patterns of \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) and \(\mathbf{MeIm}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) are consistent with that of parent material MOF-808, indicating the framework structure was retained after the encapsulation of Im or MeIm molecules. The composites were first analyzed using Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy as presented in Figure S3. Compared with the FT-IR spectrum of MOF-808, several new bands appeared in the spectra of the composites, which correspond to the characteristic peaks of the encapsulated molecules. Specifically, \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) and the bulk Im showed the same \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{N}\) stretching vibrations at 1574 and \(1320~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) as well as \(\mathrm{N-H}\) bending at \(840~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). \(\mathbf{MeIm}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) displayed \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{N}\) stretching vibrations at 1568 and \(1154~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), along with \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}\) bending at \(747~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which are characteristic peaks of the loaded MeIm molecules. The successful encapsulation of Im or MeIm in MOF-808 was
further confirmed by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm measurements at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\). As shown in Figure \(^{3\mathrm{b-d},}\) MOF-808 has a Brunauer− Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area of \(1702\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and a pore volume of \(0.67~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Upon encapsulation of Im or MeIm, the BET surface areas and pore volumes were decreased to 23 \(\mathbf{m}^{2}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}\) and \(0.01~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\), and \(122\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(0.05\ \ c\mathrm{m}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for \(\mathbf{MeIm}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\), respectively. To quantify the amount of the Im or MeIm molecules in the final materials, nuclear magnetic resonance \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) NMR) spectroscopy was performed on the digested composites in \(\mathrm{KOH}/\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) solution. As presented in Figure S4, \(\mathrm{^{1}H}\) NMR results revealed that there were approximately 12.2 Im and 9.4 MeIm molecules encapsulated per MOF-808 formula unit for \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) and \(\mathbf{MeIm}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\), respectively. Furthermore, the amount of loaded Im or MeIm molecules were also quantified using thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). As shown in Figure S7, the TGA curve of \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) showed a weight loss of \(38\%\), corresponding to the liberation of Im molecules upon heating. Similarly, a weight loss of \(35\%\) was observed for \(\mathbf{MeIm}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\), attributed to the release of MeIm molecules under elevated temperatures. For \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) and \(\mathbf{MeIm}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\), the weight losses determined by TGA (\(38\%\) and \(35\%\)) are in line with the calculated values of \(35\%\) and \(33\%\), respectively, based on NMR data.
To assess the catalytic activities of \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) and \(\mathbf{MeIm}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) toward the degradation of nerve agents, both composites were utilized to catalyze the hydrolysis of a V-agent simulant, DMNP, in pure water. The reaction progress was monitored using \({}^{31}\mathrm{P}\) NMR spectroscopy by tracking the integrals of the DMNP peak (\(-4.4~\mathrm{ppm}\)) and the peak of the hydrolyzed product, dimethyl phosphate (DMP) (\(2.8~\mathrm{ppm}\)). The hydrolysis profiles of DMNP catalyzed by \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) and \(\mathbf{MeIm}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) with \(6~\mathrm{mol}~\%\) catalyst loading (relative to DMNP) in pure water were shown in Figure 4, and representative \({}^{31}\mathrm{P}\) NMR spectra were included in Figure S9 and Figure S10. Remarkably, \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) exhibited a very fast hydrolysis rate of DMNP and reached a \(54\%\) conversion within \(30~\mathrm{s}\) (\(t_{1/2}<30\mathrm{s}\), \(\mathrm{TOF}=0.30~\mathrm{s}^{-1}\)), which was almost the
same activity as MOF-808 in NEM buffered solution.16 A control experiment was performed by using the parent MOF808 as the catalyst for the hydrolysis of DMNP in pure water, and a fairly low conversion of \(24\%\) was observed after \(2\,\mathrm{~h~}\) (Figure. S8). This suggests that the encapsulated Im molecules in MOF-808 indeed served as efficient bases for DMNP hydrolysis as we expected. In comparison with \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808},\) MeIm@MOF-808 displayed inferior catalytic performance toward the hydrolysis of DMNP \(\left(t_{1/2}=10\mathrm{\min},\,T O F=0.014\right.\) \(\mathbf{s}^{-1}\) ) as shown in Figure 4. After a relatively fast initial hydrolysis rate of DMNP, the MeIm@MOF-808-catalyzed reaction reached only \(67\%\) conversion after \(1\,\mathrm{{h}}\) . The slowdown of the hydrolysis can be attributed to the effects of product inhibition in which hydrolysis products anchor to the \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) nodes and hinder further catalysis.32 Since Im and MeIm have similar \(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}\) values \((\sim\!14.5)\) , the \(\mathrm{pH}\) measurements for the MOF catalyst suspensions before the DMNP hydrolysis process indicated close \(\mathrm{pH}\) values for \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) (7.43) and MeIm@MOF-808 (7.50). Thus, the greater catalytic activity of \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) as compared with MeIm@MOF-808 is probably due to the former's higher content of imidazole moieties (12.2 for \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) vs. 9.4 for MeIm@MOF808), consequently providing more accessible proximate bases to promote the proton transfer and the regeneration of \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) active sites during the hydrolysis.17,28,29,33,34
We next compared the performance of \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) and MeIm@MOF-808 with previously reported MOF-based catalysts for the hydrolysis of nerve agent simulants in pure water (without the liquid base NEM), and the results are summarized in Table 1 (including single-component MOFbased catalysts only) and Table S1 (including all MOF-based catalysts). With a \(6\;\mathrm{mol}\;\%\) catalyst loading, \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) and MeIm@MOF-808 can hydrolyze DMNP in pure water with half-lives of less than \(30~\mathrm{s}\) and \(10\ \mathrm{min}_{\mathrm{}}\) , respectively (Table 1). Remarkably, the catalytic activity of \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) is superior to both amine-functionalized and metal alkoxide-modified ZrMOFs.22−25 As shown in Table 1, with a \(15\%\) catalyst loading, the more efficient amine-functionalized \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOF, NU-901- \(\left[\mathbf{BA-CH}_{2}\mathbf{NH}_{2}\right]_{2},^{22}\) displayed a half-life of \(5~\mathrm{\min}\) for the hydrolysis of DMNP in pure water, which is much slower than \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) . Furthermore, \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs incorporated with metal alkoxides, \(\mathbf{MOF-808}@\mathbf{Mg}(\mathbf{OMe})_{2}\) and UiO-66− \(\mathbf{0.25NH}_{2}(\mathbf{a}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{i}\mathbf{O}^{\mathbf{t}}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{u},\) were reported to degrade a nerve agent simulant diisopropylfluorophosphate (DIFP) in unbuffered
aqueous solution with half-lives of 6.8 and \(0.4~\mathrm{\min},\) respectively.23,24 However, higher catalyst loadings were used in these studies ( \(50\%\) and \(100\%\) for \(\mathbf{MOF-808}@\mathbf{Mg}(\mathbf{OMe})_{2}\) and \(\mathbf{UiO-66-0.25NH_{2}@L i O^{t}B u},\) respectively). Prior to this work, the most efficient MOF-based catalyst overall for nerve agent (simulant) hydrolysis in pure water was a MOF/ polyamine base mixture NU-901/PAMAM 1.0, and it achieved a half-life of \(1.1\,\mathrm{\min}\) for DMNP hydrolysis with 6 \(\mathrm{mol}\;\%\) catalyst loading.20 Under similar conditions, \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF}-\) 808 exhibited a half-life \((<\!30\ \mathrm{~s~})\) for DMNP hydrolysis, rendering it the best-performing MOF-based catalyst for the hydrolysis of nerve agent simulants in pure water (without the liquid base NEM).
Notably, water serves as an essential reactant in the catalytic hydrolysis of nerve agents/simulants, and MOFs are exceptional adsorbents for water owing to their high porosity and surface area. It was reported that MOFs are capable of adsorbing water from both humid and dry environments,35,36 which makes them the most promising material to achieve the detoxification of nerve agents under ambient conditions. However, because of the requirement of volatile liquid bases in the MOF-catalyzed nerve agent hydrolysis, most research to date focused on testing the catalysis in liquid water, and very few studies reported the solid-phase hydrolysis of nerve agents (simulants) catalyzed by MOF-based catalysts.19,26 Given the high catalytic activity of \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) for DMNP hydrolysis in pure water, we sought to investigate the catalytic hydrolysis of DMNP with \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) under high relative humidity (99% RH) (Figure 5a). Briefly, the \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) composite were first mixed with DMNP (6 mol % catalyst loading) and incubated in a 99% RH chamber for a certain time period, the mixture was then immediately digested in \(\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}/\mathrm{DMSO-D}_{6}\) for \(^{31}\mathrm{P}\) NMR measurement (Figure S13), and the conversion of DMNP was determined. By changing the reaction time for each sample, a plot of DMNP conversion vs time was generated as presented in Figure 5b. This plot showed that under 99% RH, \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) (6 mol %) degraded DMNP with \(t_{1/2}=15\) min and \(\mathrm{TOF}=0.56~\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) . It is worth noting that the catalytic activity of \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) in humid environment is comparable to that of some \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs in bulk
water buffered by NEM base.11 In contrast, MeIm@MOF808-catalyzed DMNP hydrolysis exhibited a slow reaction rate in solid phase \(\left(t_{1/2}\,=\,2\,\mathrm{h},\right.\) \(\mathrm{TOF}\,=\,4.17\,\mathrm{\h^{-1}})\) under \(99\%\) RH (Figure S14), which may be attributed to its insufficient number of proximate bases. As a comparison, the solid-phase DMNP hydrolysis catalyzed by parent MOF-808 under \(99\%\) RH showed a negligible conversion (less than \(1\%\) ) after \(24~\mathrm{h}\) (Figure S15).
In summary, using a biomimetic approach, we have successfully developed two MOF composites, \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) and MeIm \(@\) MOF-808, which closely resemble the active sites of phosphotriesterase. In unbuffered aqueous solution, \(\mathbf{Im}(\mathbf{\hat{a}}\) MOF-808 and MeIm \(@\) MOF-808 decomposed a nerve agent simulant, DMNP, with half-lives of \({<}30\) s and \(10~\mathrm{\min},\) respectively. To the best of our knowledge, the catalytic performance of \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) for pure-water DMNP hydrolysis is so far the best among all MOF-based catalysts. Remarkably, \(\mathbf{Im}@\mathbf{MOF-808}\) also achieved rapid hydrolysis of DMNP under high humidity conditions without bulk water. Our study demonstrated a simple and feasible strategy of designing highly efficient MOF-based catalysts for nerve agent detoxification without requiring liquid base, and this work serves as a significant step toward the practical applications of MOFs in personal protective equipment. We are currently working on the incorporation of these highly active MOF catalysts into polymer matrices for nerve agent detoxification under ambient conditions.
